WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:07.530
Over the past two
years, we've been

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:09.090
working on this
workflow that's been

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:11.857
helpful for us in processing
large amounts of data

00:00:11.857 --> 00:00:14.190
very quickly in order to
create products that help guide

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:17.020
the decision making in the state
of where to allocate resources.

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:19.860
This workflow involves three
main products Microsoft Azure

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.297
DevOps, RStudio
Workbench, and RStudio Connect.

00:00:22.297 --> 00:00:23.880
If you're at the
keynote this morning,

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:25.380
you'll realize
some of these terms

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:30.260
are a little outdated, since
we're now changing to Posit.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:33.732
When I first started working
at CDP, it was December 2020.

00:00:33.732 --> 00:00:35.690
I was living in Washington,
DC in Foggy Bottom,

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:36.960
so not too far from here.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.752
And if you remember,
in our personal lives,

00:00:38.752 --> 00:00:39.720
it was quite stressful.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.803
So going outside was kind
of like rolling the dice

00:00:41.803 --> 00:00:44.063
on whether you might get
sick or get other sick.

00:00:44.063 --> 00:00:46.230
And you can imagine working
in public health is also

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:47.320
quite stressful.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:49.770
Our infrastructure wasn't
prepared for the amount of data

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:52.200
that COVID was going to
provide, and we're often trying

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.150
to catch up and get ahead.

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:55.650
I happened to be
working on a report

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:58.560
at that time that got sent
out at the end of the day.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:00.150
And I would get
this beautiful data

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:02.382
set that was processed
by many colleagues

00:01:02.382 --> 00:01:03.840
from the local
health jurisdictions

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.050
and at the state level.

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:07.470
And sometimes there would
be delays to no fault

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:09.760
that anyone the situation
is very complicated,

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:11.640
data was always
changing. So this often

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:16.600
meant 9:00 pm, 10 p.m., 11:00
pm, California time, midnight,

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:18.393
1:00 AM 2:00 AM in DC.

00:01:18.393 --> 00:01:19.560
So I have to look like this.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:23.580
I was very tired and my cat was
wondering why I'm still awake.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:24.192
As a team,

00:01:24.192 --> 00:01:25.650
we knew this wasn't
feasible and we

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:26.850
needed to do something
to change this,

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:28.517
not only to meet the
needs of the state,

00:01:28.517 --> 00:01:31.170
but also to improve
our work life balance.

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:33.420
So eventually this workflow
came about allowing

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:36.000
us to do our work
more efficiently,

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.390
and I got into bed at
a more reasonable time.

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:42.010
So reducing our stress and
required four main things.

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:45.140
The first one is how
we prioritize our task.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:48.560
How do we improve our ability
to collaborate with each other?

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.810
How do we decrease
our turnaround time?

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:54.560
And then lastly, how
do we scale our work?

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:56.240
So I'm going to
start with how do we

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:57.480
improve our prioritization?

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:00.182
So during this time there were
many requests was coming in

00:02:00.182 --> 00:02:02.640
and there's often the undertone
of I needed this yesterday.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:05.660
And that's very true because
the questions and issues

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:08.780
that were having impacted the
health of millions of people.

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:12.180
And the issue, however, is that
as a team we're quite limited.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:13.520
There's only so many of us.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:18.247
Our skills vary and we want to
be able to solve these issues,

00:02:18.247 --> 00:02:19.830
but we only have so
much time as well.

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:21.650
So we needed a way
to prioritize how

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:23.458
we move forward in our work.

00:02:23.458 --> 00:02:25.250
So that's where the
first product comes in,

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:28.270
Microsoft Azure DevOps, and
we use Microsoft Azure DevOps

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:30.110
at CDP because we're
a Microsoft shop.

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:32.350
But you could use
other DevOps platforms as well

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:34.640
in place of this.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:37.190
So we started using Azure
DevOps for project management,

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:39.390
in particular a sprint board.

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:41.540
And a sprint is a
defined period of time

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:43.672
that you set to complete
a number of tasks.

00:02:43.672 --> 00:02:45.380
And what's really
great about a sprint is

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:47.180
that at the very
beginning of the sprint,

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:50.990
it forces you to sit down and
decide what needs to be done,

00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:53.630
what can realistically be
done in this time period,

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:55.310
and who's going to do it.

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:57.830
And for us, we happen to
use a two week sprint time,

00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:00.110
but you could use different
amount of time as well.

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:01.550
So what I'm going to
do next is show you

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:03.133
what our sprint
board looks like,

00:03:03.133 --> 00:03:05.140
to give you a sense of that.

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:07.120
So we have five
columns features, not

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:10.972
started, declined,
in progress, and done.

00:03:10.972 --> 00:03:12.430
In the feature
column, you're going

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:14.000
to have an overarching project.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.618
So, for example, preparing
for this RStudio conference talk,

00:03:16.618 --> 00:03:18.910
and within that, you're going
to have a number of tasks

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:20.850
to complete within your sprint.

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:22.740
So in the not
started, I might have

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:25.990
tasks about drafting slides or
practicing this presentation.

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:28.080
And you can be the judge
of whether I successfully

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:30.300
did these or not.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:32.640
I may have declined columns
such as adding cat GIFs

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:34.530
and at some point
realized or decided

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:36.610
this wasn't a professional
enough thing to add.

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:38.877
So I'm not going to do that.

00:03:38.877 --> 00:03:41.460
I'll have an in-progress column
such as drafting outline, which

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:43.043
I need to do prior
to doing the slides

00:03:43.043 --> 00:03:44.885
or practicing my presentation.

00:03:44.885 --> 00:03:47.010
And then lastly, I'll finish
a couple of tasks too.

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:48.720
So such as making
this sprint board example,

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:51.030
which again, you can judge
of whether I successfully

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:51.977
did that or not.

00:03:51.977 --> 00:03:54.060
And then we'll have other
rows with other features

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:54.940
for separate tasks.

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:57.270
And this provides a
really great visualization

00:03:57.270 --> 00:04:00.220
for our team of how we're
doing in this sprint.

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:02.550
And what's really powerful
is that as sprints continue

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:04.350
to occur, we can
look back in time

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:06.570
and see how successful we
were at completing our task

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:08.910
and modify how we're
going to move forward

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:10.170
in the next sprint.

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:12.570
And if you're disappointed
that I didn't add any gifts,

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:16.380
I added some last
minute for you.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:18.579
So next, how did we
improve our collaboration?

00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:21.810
So when I first started, we were
using Git and DevOps principles

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:24.310
in some places, but it wasn't
a main part of our workflow.

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:28.780
So a lot of our work was shared
across a shared drive or folder

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:32.453
in at work, and that often
meant copying your R script,

00:04:32.453 --> 00:04:33.870
for example, and
then making edits

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:35.340
and later putting
it back together,

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:37.747
which over time was starting
to make a lot of problems

00:04:37.747 --> 00:04:40.080
especially when it came down to
production, or it was late at night

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:43.440
at midnight and edits
were just very frustrating

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:45.320
and tiring to do.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:47.960
And so we started to use Git
principle, or Git workflow

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:52.440
more effectively and made it
a main part of our workflow.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:54.853
So if you think about our
work and RStudio Workbench,

00:04:54.853 --> 00:04:56.270
it's kind of like
we're in a silo.

00:04:56.270 --> 00:04:57.728
It's really hard
to share this work

00:04:57.728 --> 00:04:59.340
with others or the
colleagues, and we

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:01.490
wanted to be able to do that.

00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:04.230
So that's where we started using
repositories via Azure DevOps.

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:06.200
And a repository
is a place, a place

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:09.170
where you can store your code
safely, have version control,

00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:13.270
and it's this central place
that all of your work can live.

00:05:13.270 --> 00:05:15.550
And what's great about
this is that via RStudio

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:17.650
and Git, we can move our work

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:20.290
outside of our silo
into this repository.

00:05:20.290 --> 00:05:22.690
And in that same vein, I
can move other people's work

00:05:22.690 --> 00:05:27.047
from the repositories into my
Workbench and work on it there.

00:05:27.047 --> 00:05:28.880
Now you can have an
entire workshop about Git

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:30.550
and still may not understand it.

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:32.910
It's very confusing, but
there's a really great resource

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:34.990
by Jenny Bryan
called Happy Git

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:37.760
that's related to using Git in R
that we encourage you to check

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:40.855
out if you want to
get started with Git and R.

00:05:40.855 --> 00:05:42.730
So you can think of
repositories as if you're

00:05:42.730 --> 00:05:44.190
storing your code safely.

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:46.090
So in these photos
by Eadweard Muybridge,

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:48.370
we're seeing this
the spirit in flight

00:05:48.370 --> 00:05:50.680
and we're getting snapshots,
shots, snapshots of it

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:51.293
over time.

00:05:51.293 --> 00:05:52.960
And you can think of
our code every time

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:54.490
we make a commit
or a save, we're

00:05:54.490 --> 00:05:58.120
saving a version of it in
memory, in the repository.

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:00.850
So maybe you want to put this
last snapshot into production,

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:02.830
but something goes wrong.

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:05.800
What's great about repository is
that we can roll back the clock

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:07.300
and use this previous
version to go

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:08.800
into production and
in the meantime,

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:13.240
fix our errors that
occurred later on.

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:14.890
And another really
great feature is

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:16.750
that you can make
what's called a fork

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:19.567
or a branch of a repository,
and that's like making a copy of it.

00:06:19.567 --> 00:06:21.400
And you can kind of
think of it as if you're

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:22.210
working in your own land.

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:23.950
In that sense, we're all
running in the sprint

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:25.750
and I have my own
fork or branch and I'm

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:27.400
working at making edits.

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:29.003
And if I happen
to make a mistake,

00:06:29.003 --> 00:06:30.920
it's not going to affect
others in their lane.

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:32.628
So it gives us a really
great opportunity

00:06:32.628 --> 00:06:36.557
to try new things out,
fall over, break the app,

00:06:36.557 --> 00:06:38.140
all of those things
that we don't want

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:41.560
to happen during production.

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:43.630
And lastly, how to
improve or turnaround

00:06:43.630 --> 00:06:44.950
time and scalability?

00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:47.650
So we're always getting
requests and lots of data

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:51.700
that we're taking ownership of,
and these data processing can

00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:52.638
take hours.

00:06:52.638 --> 00:06:55.180
And that same time, we also want
to be able to scale our work

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:57.205
and share it with others.

00:06:57.205 --> 00:06:58.580
So that's where
our final product

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:00.150
comes in, RStudio Connect.

00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:02.817
And this is a server that allows
you to automate your processes.

00:07:02.817 --> 00:07:04.900
And what's really cool is
that from our repositories

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:06.770
in Azure DevOps,
we can deploy our work

00:07:06.770 --> 00:07:09.290
into RStudio Connect.

00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:11.900
And from Connect, we can
automate data processing.

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:14.190
We can schedule reports.

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:17.440
And we can post Shiny apps.

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:19.810
So RStudio Connect provided
us more processing power,

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:22.550
which allowed us to do
analyses more quickly.

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:24.740
So just as an example,
this is one of our servers.

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:28.120
We have about 251 gigabytes
of ram, which is a lot.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:30.940
Or another example, the
amount of CPU cores available.

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:33.370
And we have a number of
these servers available to us

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:35.740
to do different types of
data processing or host apps.

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:38.350
And I can't explain
the giddiness

00:07:38.350 --> 00:07:40.660
I felt the first time I ran
a script on Connect that

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:42.790
took 10 minutes when it
used to take over an hour

00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:44.510
to run if it didn't crash.

00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:46.330
So Connect was a
really great lifesaver

00:07:46.330 --> 00:07:48.490
in terms of finishing our
data processing in more

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:52.270
quickly, quick manner allowing
me to go to sleep earlier.

00:07:52.270 --> 00:07:54.645
So it's not only quicker, but
it also freed up our hands.

00:07:54.645 --> 00:07:56.062
At least in our
environment, we're

00:07:56.062 --> 00:07:57.410
limited to two workbenches.

00:07:57.410 --> 00:08:00.095
So that's kind of like having
two R consoles or two tabs.

00:08:00.095 --> 00:08:01.720
And if I have many
types of data that I

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:03.220
need to process
throughout the week,

00:08:03.220 --> 00:08:04.520
it's as if my hands are tied.

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:06.550
So BY being able to offload
it off to a server,

00:08:06.550 --> 00:08:10.430
I can free up my hands to
do other type of work.

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:12.420
And we can schedule
jobs on the server.

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:15.650
I'm using, for example, the
officer or openxls package

00:08:15.650 --> 00:08:19.490
to make PowerPoints Excel
sheets or Word documents.

00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:21.920
And in that same
data processing,

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:24.682
we can also be doing the
data, the large amount of data

00:08:24.682 --> 00:08:26.390
processing and then
creating reports that

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:29.110
are output at a scheduled time.

00:08:29.110 --> 00:08:31.432
And we can schedule emails
with the blastula package

00:08:31.432 --> 00:08:33.640
and a couple of emails here
and there is not too bad.

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:36.610
But when we start to
initiate emails and data

00:08:36.610 --> 00:08:38.020
with a lot of
different partners,

00:08:38.020 --> 00:08:41.390
this becomes a really
great tool to use.

00:08:41.390 --> 00:08:43.340
And here's one example
of data that we

00:08:43.340 --> 00:08:46.263
put on the website weekly for
post vaccination infection data

00:08:46.263 --> 00:08:47.430
if you want to check it out.

00:08:47.430 --> 00:08:49.280
And all of this data is
processed via Connect

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:50.655
and then shared
with our partners

00:08:50.655 --> 00:08:53.642
at CDP to later
publish on the website.

00:08:53.642 --> 00:08:55.100
So you can kind of
think of Connect

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:56.310
as if they're replacing us.

00:08:56.310 --> 00:08:57.680
They're robots, they're
running in their own lane

00:08:57.680 --> 00:08:59.533
and they're running
their own processes.

00:08:59.533 --> 00:09:00.950
And so at this
point, I might want

00:09:00.950 --> 00:09:03.050
to sit back and look at
my cat and listen to her

00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:05.090
and stop looking
at this monitor.

00:09:05.090 --> 00:09:07.910
But in reality, automation
isn't perfect and often

00:09:07.910 --> 00:09:11.060
actually fails and can be
quite challenging to debug.

00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:12.918
Working on a server,
the debugging process

00:09:12.918 --> 00:09:13.710
is a lot different.

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:14.850
The logs are different.

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:16.615
So we try to.

00:09:16.615 --> 00:09:18.240
So we need to think
about that as well.

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:18.780
So in reality,

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:19.613
it's more like this.

00:09:19.613 --> 00:09:21.230
We're all running
in the same lane.

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:24.080
Some of the processes are
falling over, I'm falling over,

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:26.160
et cetera.

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:29.730
And one of the solutions to this
was to create a data processing

00:09:29.730 --> 00:09:30.690
monitoring app.

00:09:30.690 --> 00:09:33.357
So this monitors all
the processes on Connect

00:09:33.357 --> 00:09:35.190
and it gives us a really
great visualization

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:36.460
of what's occurring.

00:09:36.460 --> 00:09:37.830
And when it might fail.

00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:40.590
And in addition to that, we
have an error logging system

00:09:40.590 --> 00:09:44.460
where if something breaks, we
get an email via blastula telling us

00:09:44.460 --> 00:09:46.870
something's broken and where
in that script it broke.

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:49.120
So I don't have time to share
that with you right now.

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:50.280
But if you're
interested, there's

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:52.380
actually a talk by my
colleagues, Kolbi Parrish,

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:54.463
and Andy Pham coming up next
that you could check out

00:09:54.463 --> 00:09:57.690
to see that specific
piece of our workflow.

00:09:57.690 --> 00:09:59.997
And then lastly, we have
many different Shiny apps,

00:09:59.997 --> 00:10:01.830
mostly internal that I
can't share with you,

00:10:01.830 --> 00:10:05.160
but one that was published to
the public by the California

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:07.200
COVID-19 modeling
team called calcat.

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:09.060
And this shows you
the current state

00:10:09.060 --> 00:10:12.150
and predicted future state using
different models of COVID 19

00:10:12.150 --> 00:10:13.500
in the State of California.

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:15.000
So I encourage you
to check this out

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:17.260
and see the different types of
data that we're working with.

00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:19.747
And what's, I think, really cool is
that you can download the data

00:10:19.747 --> 00:10:21.330
as well that we've
all been processing

00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:24.770
and it's updated regularly.

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:26.750
So this gives you a
very high level overview

00:10:26.750 --> 00:10:29.460
of the different tools that we
use to create this workflow.

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:32.210
Using Azure DevOps, RStudio
Workbench, and RStudio

00:10:32.210 --> 00:10:34.280
Connect, allowing
us to process data

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:35.573
in a very quick amount of time.

00:10:35.573 --> 00:10:37.490
And freeing up our hands
to make products that

00:10:37.490 --> 00:10:39.620
help guide decision
making in the state

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:41.683
of where to allocate resources.

00:10:41.683 --> 00:10:44.100
Thank you all for your time
and special thanks to the data

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:47.720
processing and informatics
section for all our hard work.

